From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 02:54:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Iterate cursors over indexes from their position
 in the index

A cursor over an IDBIndex iterated the records of the index's object
store: Iterate had no index, the backend read the store's records in
the cursor's range, all of them for an index source, and script's steps
to iterate a cursor searched that list. Index keys, the order by primary
key within a key, nextunique and prevunique, continuePrimaryKey() and
the object store position never worked, and every step of an index
cursor read the whole range with its values.

Iterate now names the index, like the other reads, and carries the
direction and the step (IndexedDBCursorStep): the cursor's position and
object store position, the key of continue() or continuePrimaryKey(),
the primary key of the latter and the count of advance(). The backend
runs step 9 of "iterate a cursor" with one statement per step, on the
records as they are when the step runs (queries::cursor_records, with
the spec's requirements in its comments):

- It bounds the records on the side the cursor moves to by the tightest
  of the range's bound, the key and the position, so that the statement
  seeks there in the table's primary key order (StepBounds). For an index
  in "next" and "prev" the position, and continuePrimaryKey()'s target,
  are (key, primary key) row values, `(i.value, i.object_data_key) >
  (?, ?)`; the other requirements bound the key alone.
- advance(n) skips n - 1 records, or keys for the unique directions,
  with OFFSET.
- "nextunique" and "prevunique" on a non-unique index keep, of each
  key's records, the one with the lowest primary key, in both
  directions, as the spec's "first record whose key is equal to temp
  record's key" asks for "prevunique": a NOT EXISTS condition with a
  primary key seek per record. Not MIN(object_data_key) with GROUP BY:
  RDB can't read a column that holds the MIN() or MAX() of BLOBs. A
  unique index has one record per key and needs no condition.

Script sends the step (IDBCursor::iterate_operation) and applies the
record it gets back (iterate_cursor, steps 9.2 to 15). Object store
cursors take the same path, so the narrowing that b17fe190449 did in
script for them now happens in the backend.

The reply lists records in step order, at most `records` of them: the
one the step finds, then those that continue() without a key would find
next. Script asks for one; the prefetch cache planned for later can ask
for more without a protocol change. Key cursors (openKeyCursor) ask for
no values, so their steps don't read object_data, and
IndexedDBRecord.value is optional.

Cursor update() and delete() already acted on the effective object
store and recomputed the index keys; with the index records in place
they now show in the index's iteration.

Tests: backend tests check every step against a literal implementation
of the spec's step 9 over the same records, for a store and a
non-unique, a unique and a multiEntry index, in every direction and in
four ranges, from every position, with continue(), advance(),
continue(key), continuePrimaryKey() and several records per step, and
with random puts and deletes between the steps of cursors over the store
and two indexes; the integration test of b17fe190449 now sends steps.
The fork WPT test index-cursors.any.js covers what the IndexedDB WPT
tests leave out: the unique directions on keys with several records,
continuePrimaryKey() and advance() across them, writes between the
steps of an index cursor, a unique index whose key moves to another
record, and update() and delete() through an index cursor with
generated keys.

Validation: Before the P3 review fixes were folded into the P3.1, P3.2 and P3.3 commits: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 139 passed, 1 skipped (P3.3: 137), including test_cursor_steps_find_what_the_spec_finds (4,726 steps checked against the spec's algorithm) and test_cursor_steps_see_the_writes_between_them. DAYU200 `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage`: RDB only 140 passed, 3 skipped (138); SQLite and RDB 249 passed, 4 skipped (245); a first version of the unique directions with GROUP BY and MIN(object_data_key) failed there. WPT IndexedDB/ (4,511 subtests): host SpiderMonkey, 8 processes, 3,971 pass (P3.3: 3,796); QuickJS 3,971 (3,797), 820 of 820 tests as expected; DAYU200 (QuickJS + RDB), 2 processes, 3,952 (3,773). The 174 new passes are the same on both host engines (idbcursor-continue, -advance, -primarykey, -continuePrimaryKey and its exception tests, -direction-index and -keyrange, -iterating-update, -advance-continue-async, idbcursor_continue_index, idbcursor_update_index, idbindex_tombstones, idbindex_reverse_cursor, idbindex_keyPath arrays, name-scopes, large-requests-abort, request-event-ordering-*, reading-autoincrement-indexes-cursors, index_sort_order, idbobjectstore_createIndex and idb-partitioned-coverage cursor subtests); the board has them except idb-partitioned-coverage, which times out as a whole there since P3.3 because pages in iframes run slowly on the board. The other unexpected results are known: order-dependent flips of worker and sharedworker variants that share database names (each passes alone), and interleaved-cursors-large and file_support on the board. Board durations of the object store cursor tests are unchanged: idbcursor_iterating 3.9-4.3 s (4.1-4.4), interleaved-cursors-small 26.6-29.1 s (27.0-30.2). /_mozilla/IndexedDB/: 172 of 172 on host SpiderMonkey, QuickJS and the board; the P3.3 binary passes none of the 21 subtests of index-cursors.any.js. `./mach fmt`; `./mach test-tidy`: only the known libsqlite3-sys advisory; `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release`, and `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --locked --ohos --profile checked-release` for the default and harmonyos flavors, each with a signed HAP: no warning; `./mach test-unit --profile checked-release`: 1,342 passed, 1 skipped, after a first run that failed the randomized layout test floats::test_floats_rule_8; with --doc: pass. After them, at this commit: host nextest 140 passed, 1 skipped; DAYU200 RDB only 141 passed / 3 skipped, SQLite and RDB 251 / 4; WPT IndexedDB/ host SpiderMonkey 3,970 and QuickJS 3,970, each 819 of 820 tests as expected (idbfactory-origin-isolation on SM, the open.blocked flip of idbversionchangeevent.any.worker on QuickJS, both known), DAYU200 3,953 with the known unexpected set; /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 187 of 187 on host SM, QuickJS and the board; `./mach fmt`, test-tidy (only the known advisory), clippy, the crown build, both OHOS flavors with signed HAPs, `./mach test-unit --profile checked-release` (1,343 passed, 1 skipped) and --doc pass.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change and the tests, and ran the host, device and WPT runs, again after the P3 review fold)
